    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-23849",
      "knowledge_point_id": "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we operate the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
